          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING AUTHENTICATION on the entire /v1/datasets/* subrouter. `publicRoutes` in authMiddleware contains the unanchored regex `new RegExp('/v1/datasets/.+')` (auth/utils.ts:76). The datasets Router has prefix \"/datasets\" (datasets/index.ts:9) mounted on the v1 router with prefix \"/v1\" (api/v1/index.ts:18,39), so every path /v1/datasets/<anything> matches. In authMiddleware, when `isPublicRoute` is true the function calls `await next(); return` at lines 94 and 104 WITHOUT ever calling verifyJwt and WITHOUT reaching the project-ownership check at line 120 (`select id from project where id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and org_id = ${ctx.state.orgId}`). Worse, line 82 sets `ctx.state.projectId = ctx.request?.query?.projectId` and line 92 sets `ctx.state.projectId = bearerToken` for any UUID bearer, so an unauthenticated attacker fully controls the tenant identifier the handlers trust. Only `GET /v1/datasets` and `POST /v1/datasets` escape (the `.+` requires a char after the slash). I confirmed authMiddleware (index.ts:34) is the ONLY auth gate: the sole `.use()` calls under packages/backend/src/api are `routes()` mounts plus `runs.use(\"/ingest\", ...)`, i.e. no per-router auth guard exists as a compensating control.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts:76 places the unanchored regex `new RegExp(`/v1/datasets/.+`)` in the `publicRoutes` array (L62-78). In authMiddleware (L81-106), `isPublicRoute` is computed by `route.test(ctx.path)` (L84-86); when true, BOTH L90-96 and L98-106 execute `await next(); return` without ever calling `verifyJwt` (L112) and without reaching the tenancy check at L120-126 (`select id from project where id = projectId and org_id = orgId`). Worse, L82 sets `ctx.state.projectId` from `ctx.request.query.projectId` and L92/L102 set it from the raw bearer token, so an unauthenticated attacker fully controls the tenant identifier the handlers trust. Path math verified in source: index.ts:34 `app.use(authMiddleware)` and index.ts:41 `app.use(v1.routes())` -> v1/index.ts:18 prefix \"/v1\" -> v1/index.ts:39 `v1.use(datasets.routes())` -> datasets/index.ts:9 prefix \"/datasets\", and because the middleware is app-level (pre-routing) ctx.path is the full `/v1/datasets/...`. I verified no compensating control myself: my own grep for `.use(`/`authMiddleware` across packages/backend/src yields only `routes()` mounts plus `runs.use(\"/ingest\",...)` and `webhooks.use(stripe.routes())`, and utils/ratelimit.ts is a pure koa-ratelimit, not an auth gate. The lead's exemption claim is also correct: `.+` requires a char after the trailing slash so bare `/v1/datasets` (GET/POST) still gets full JWT auth. The impact is WORSE than claimed: several datasets handlers apply no projectId filter at all, so no tenant id even needs to be guessed -- datasets/index.ts:111-117 runs `delete from dataset where id = ${id}` (unauthenticated arbitrary cross-tenant dataset deletion) and :149-163 `GET /prompts/:id` returns any prompt plus its variations; :120, :166, :176, :188, :202, :211, :243 are likewise unscoped. Graph corroborates: the authMiddleware body (L84-112) is reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and getDatasetById/getDatasetBySlug are EntryPoints with exposure=\"reachable\". The datasets route lambdas report reachable_from_entry=false, but that is the documented koa-router arrow-function under-linking (dynamic dispatch), and the source mount chain proves HTTP reach, so I did not downgrade on that flag. Advisory exploit_search was unavailable (corpus not indexed), which does not affect the verdict since this is a first-party code defect rather than a dependency-version CVE."
          },
