        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING ORG-OWNERSHIP CHECK on project deletion. `projects.delete(\"/:projectId\")` (projects/index.ts:70) reads `projectId` from ctx.params, then counts projects belonging to the CALLER's own org (`select count(*)::int from project where org_id = ${orgId}`) and, if that count > 1, executes `delete from project where id = ${projectId}` with NO `org_id` predicate. The guard only validates the caller's own org has a spare project; it never validates that the target project belongs to that org. authMiddleware's project-ownership check (auth/utils.ts:120) only validates `ctx.state.projectId` which comes from the QUERY STRING (auth/utils.ts:82) \u2014 it does not cover `ctx.params.projectId`. So any authenticated user whose org holds >=2 projects can delete any project in any other organization (destructive cross-tenant data loss).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. The DELETE /v1/projects/:projectId handler takes projectId from ctx.params (projects/index.ts:71) and executes `delete from project where id = ${projectId}` with NO org_id predicate (:78); the only guard counts projects in the CALLER's own org (:75), which validates nothing about the target's ownership. The global authMiddleware does not cover this: ctx.state.projectId is populated solely from ctx.request.query.projectId (auth/utils.ts:82) and the ownership query at :121 is gated behind `if (ctx.state.projectId)` (:117), so a request with no query string skips the check entirely, and a request with `?projectId=<attacker's own>` passes the check while the DELETE still targets the victim's path param. Structurally the middleware CANNOT check the path param: it is registered app-level (index.ts:34) before the router (index.ts:41), so ctx.params is unpopulated when it runs. No other gate exists \u2014 projects.routes() is mounted bare (api/v1/index.ts:37) and grep for checkAccess|hasAccess|requireRole|ctx.state.role across packages/backend/src returns zero matches. The codebase's own secure pattern was omitted here: templates.ts:79 correctly scopes its delete by `project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId}`. Impact is destructive and cascading (run/template/radar/api_key/external_user/evaluation/checklist all FK with `on delete cascade`); the single non-cascading FK (`log`, init.sql:242) cannot abort it because the log table is never read or written anywhere in the backend. The graph marks the sink reachable_from_entry=false/hop_distance=-1, but that is the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact \u2014 the source proves the route is live (app.use(v1.routes()) at index.ts:41, server listening at :46) and /v1/projects is absent from publicRoutes (auth/utils.ts:62-78), i.e. a real JWT-authenticated endpoint. One refinement to the analyst's claim: the `count > 1` gate (:77) requires the attacker's own org to hold >=2 projects \u2014 a fresh free-tier signup gets exactly one project (auth/index.ts:69-75) and projects.post 403s on the free plan (:41-43), so exploitation requires a non-free plan, a self-hosted deploy with DEFAULT_PLAN set (auth/index.ts:51), or membership in an already-multi-project org. The sibling projects.patch(\"/:projectId\") (:91-106) has the same missing org_id predicate with no precondition at all, letting any authenticated user rename any org's project. Exploit catalog was unindexed, so it contributed no signal either way; verdict rests entirely on first-party source evidence."
          },
