        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING AUTHORIZATION CHECK ENTIRELY on project rename. `projects.patch(\"/:projectId\")` (projects/index.ts:91) takes `projectId` from ctx.params and runs `update project set name = ${name} where id = ${projectId}` with no org_id predicate and without reading ctx.state.orgId at all \u2014 the handler never references the caller's org or role. Any authenticated user can rename any project in any organization. The safe pattern exists in sibling handlers in the same file (projects.get(\"/\") line 10 and projects.post(\"/\") line 31 both scope on `ctx.state.orgId`), confirming the omission.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. The PATCH /v1/projects/:projectId handler takes projectId from ctx.params and executes `update project set name = ${name} where id = ${projectId}` with no org_id predicate, and never reads ctx.state.orgId. I specifically tested the strongest counter-hypothesis (that upstream middleware compensates) and it fails: authMiddleware does contain a project-ownership check (auth/utils.ts:117-127) but it guards only ctx.state.projectId, which line 82 populates from ctx.request.query.projectId (the QUERY STRING), whereas the handler reads the PATH param. An attacker omits ?projectId= entirely, so the `if (ctx.state.projectId)` guard is falsy and no ownership check runs. A grep of the entire backend for checkAccess|hasAccess|requireRole|ctx.state.role returned no matches, so no RBAC layer exists. The route is reachable: index.ts:41 app.use(v1.routes()) -> v1/index.ts:37 v1.use(projects.routes()) -> prefix /projects -> patch(\"/:projectId\"), behind authMiddleware, so any valid JWT suffices. I did NOT accept the graph's reachable_from_entry=false: a query showed every call in the file is false, including the demonstrably-live get(\"/\") line 10 and post(\"/\") line 31, proving it is the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact rather than evidence of unreachability. The safe pattern in siblings (line 11/25 org_id = ${orgId}; line 32) confirms the omission is an error. Exploitability is amplified because the target identifier is not secret: projects/index.ts:56 and auth/index.ts:80 set apiKey: project.id for the PUBLIC key (exposed as public_api_key at line 20), so project UUIDs are shipped in client-side SDKs by design. Severity MEDIUM not HIGH because the unauthorized cross-tenant write is confined to the zod-validated `name` field (defacement, not disclosure/privilege-gain/destruction). Exploit-reference check was unusable (corpus not indexed) and inapplicable: this is first-party app logic, not a versioned component, so no version-range/patched determination was made."
          },
