        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-640",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "PASSWORD-RESET TOKEN IS NEVER VALIDATED AGAINST THE STORED recovery_token AND IS NEVER INVALIDATED. `auth.post(\"/request-password-reset\")` (auth/index.ts:172) mints `signJwt({ email }, ONE_HOUR)` and persists it: `update account set recovery_token = ${token} where id = ${user.id}` (line 180). But `auth.post(\"/reset-password\")` (auth/index.ts:194) only calls `verifyJwt<{email:string}>(token)` and then immediately runs `update account set password_hash = ${passwordHash} where email = ${email} returning *` \u2014 it never compares the submitted token to `account.recovery_token`, and never clears/rotates recovery_token afterwards. I searched the whole graph for any read of recovery_token and found ONLY the write site: the stored column is write-only dead state, proving the intended single-use control is absent. Consequences: (a) a reset link is replayable for its full lifetime and still works after the password has already been changed; (b) because the only check is \"any JWT signed with JWT_SECRET that carries an `email` claim\", the session token minted by /login \u2014 `signJwt({userId, email, orgId})` with the ONE_MONTH default (auth/utils.ts:32-33) \u2014 is itself an accepted reset token, so a leaked/XSS-captured bearer token escalates from session access to permanent account takeover via password change.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts:194-218 implements POST /auth/reset-password and its ONLY authorization check is `await verifyJwt<{email:string}>(token)` (line 203); it then immediately runs `update account set password_hash = ${passwordHash} where email = ${email} returning *` (line 208). It never selects or compares the submitted token against account.recovery_token, and never clears/rotates it afterwards. A repo-wide grep for recovery_token|recoveryToken yields exactly TWO hits: the single write at auth/index.ts:180 (`update account set recovery_token = ${token} where id = ${user.id}`) and the column declaration at packages/db/init.sql:31 \u2014 the column is write-only dead state, so the intended single-use control is genuinely absent. Consequences I verified: (a) a reset link is replayable for its whole 1-hour TTL and still works after the password was already changed; (b) signJwt (auth/utils.ts:33-46) sets no aud/typ/purpose claim and verifyJwt (lines 48-53) does no audience check, so ANY JWT signed with JWT_SECRET carrying an `email` claim is accepted as a reset token \u2014 including the /login session token `signJwt({userId,email,orgId})` (index.ts:163-167) which uses the ONE_MONTH default (utils.ts:32,35). Additional vector the analyst missed: packages/backend/src/utils/emails.ts:14-16 mints `signJwt({email})` with the ONE_MONTH default and embeds it in a GET URL (/v1/users/verify-email?token=...), so that email-verification token \u2014 leaked via server/proxy logs, browser history, Referer \u2014 is a valid password-reset token for a full month. Reachability: the graph reports reachable_from_entry=false/hop_distance=-1 for all calls at lines 194-211, but that is the known Koa arrow-function under-linking artifact; source disproves it \u2014 `app.use(auth.routes())` at packages/backend/src/index.ts:42 and authMiddleware treats RegExp(`/auth/.+`) as a public route (auth/utils.ts:63,84-96), so the endpoint is unauthenticated-reachable, and the handler returns a fresh session JWT (lines 211-217) granting immediate access. exploit_search was run but the corpus is not indexed (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"); advisory only and not needed, as this is first-party logic confirmed directly in source rather than a versioned dependency. Analyst's claimed location and CWE-640 are accurate."
          },
