y param (line 82) and validated against the caller's org only when present (line 117), so an attacker simply omits it -- and the patch handler ignores it regardless. Grep for checkAccess/authorize/requireRole finds no authz layer, and utils/middleware.ts is a no-op passthrough. Two details the analyst understated: (1) init.sql:163 declares `id serial primary key`, so version IDs are sequential integers and trivially enumerable (1,2,3...), not UUIDs; (2) the secondary read-leak claim is also true -- lines 44-54 select the version unscoped and return {...version, template}, leaking foreign version content with template undefined. The graph reports reachable_from_entry=false for every call in this file including the top-level require() on line 1 and the legitimate /latest route, which is the known arrow-function under-linking artifact rather than real unreachability; the source mount chain overrides it. Exploit-reference check was not usable (corpus unindexed) and no meaningful product version can be pinned (root package.json is a generic 1.0.0 monorepo placeholder), so CVE exploitability is UNKNOWN -- immaterial, since this is a first-party logic flaw verified directly in source. Any authenticated user of any org can overwrite the content of any template version in any project/org; because these templates drive LLM calls, this is a cross-tenant prompt-tampering/injection primitive."
