enancy middleware exists in the runs router. The route is NOT in `publicRoutes` (auth/utils.ts:62-78, which whitelists only `.../public` and `.../feedback`), so a valid JWT is required -- but auth gives no protection: authMiddleware only validates org ownership of ctx.state.projectId when that query param is present (auth/utils.ts:117-127), and this handler ignores projectId entirely, so an attacker supplies their OWN valid projectId plus an arbitrary victim run id and receives the victim tenant's full run subtree. I did NOT weigh the graph's reachable_from_entry=false against exploitability, because my own query returned the same false/hop_distance=-1 for lines 163 and 189 -- and line 163 is provably live (whitelisted by name in authMiddleware) -- confirming the known arrow-function under-linking artifact affects every handler in this file uniformly and carries no signal. Corrections to the analyst: the handler closes at line 259 (not 257). Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL because run.id is `uuid default uuid_generate_v4()` (packages/db/init.sql:96), so an attacker must obtain rather than enumerate a run id -- mitigated in practice by ids leaking through shared /traces/[id] URLs, public-run pages and SDK responses, and amplified because the recursive CTE expands one known id into the entire descendant tree and discloses further parent_run_ids. Advisory exploit-reference check was attempted but the Metasploit corpus is unindexed (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\") and packages/backend/package.json carries no version field, so no version grounding was available; this is first-party application logic and the verdict rests entirely on direct source evidence."
