ngs, which identifies it as the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact rather than dead code; the real chain is handler (index.ts:72) -> `v1.use(evaluations.routes())` (api/v1/index.ts:44) -> `app.use(v1.routes())` (src/index.ts:41), and the frontend actively calls it at packages/frontend/pages/evaluations/[id].tsx:37. Auth premise holds: `app.use(authMiddleware)` (src/index.ts:34) runs before the routes and `/v1/evaluations/result/...` matches no `publicRoutes` entry (auth/utils.ts:62-78 lists only `/v1/evaluations/run`), so a valid JWT is required; `authMiddleware` validates project membership only when `ctx.state.projectId` is set from the query string (auth/utils.ts:117-127), which an attacker simply omits. Net effect: any authenticated user of any org who knows a victim evaluation id reads another tenant's evaluation outputs and prompt content. Secondary claim also verified: evaluations/utils.ts:34 `select * from checklist where id = ${checklistId}` is unscoped with checklistId from the request body (index.ts:13). I downgrade the analyst's HIGH to MEDIUM: evaluation ids are UUIDv4 (init.sql:360 `uuid_generate_v4()`), so the unguessable key requires the attacker to already know or leak a victim id. No exploit_search applied \u2014 first-party authorization logic flaw, not a versioned dependency CVE."
