          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING OWNERSHIP VERIFICATION when creating a template version. `templates.post(\"/:id/versions\")` (templates.ts:113) inserts into template_version using `template_id = ${ctx.params.id}` taken straight from the URL, without first verifying that the parent template belongs to ctx.state.projectId. Every other handler in templates.ts performs that check (line 69 get /:id, line 77 delete /:id, line 85 patch /:id all use `where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ${ctx.params.id}`), so the control is conspicuously absent only on the insert path. Any authenticated user can graft an arbitrary new version onto another tenant's prompt template. Additionally, templates.patch(\"/:id\") at line 85, after its correctly-scoped update, reads `select * from template_version where template_id = ${ctx.params.id}` unscoped, returning versions even when the scoped update matched nothing.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. templates.post(\"/:id/versions\") (templates.ts:113-132) inserts into template_version with template_id taken directly from the URL path (line 125) and performs NO verification that the parent template belongs to ctx.state.projectId, while every sibling handler in the same file does exactly that check (lines 71, 79, 95). projectId is a real tenant boundary: authMiddleware (auth/utils.ts:117-127) validates the query-string projectId against the JWT's org_id, and /v1/templates/... is not in publicRoutes (lines 62-78), so any authenticated user of ANY org can write a version row onto another tenant's template. Route is live: index.ts:34 (authMiddleware) -> index.ts:41 (app.use(v1.routes())) -> v1/index.ts:41 (v1.use(templates.routes())). No mitigating control exists: the FK template_version.template_id -> template(id) (db/init.sql:255) enforces only existence, not tenancy, and the repo has zero RLS (no matches for ROW LEVEL SECURITY / CREATE POLICY / current_setting). Two amplifiers I established myself raise severity to HIGH: (1) template.id is `serial primary key` (db/init.sql:151), so victim template IDs are sequential integers and trivially enumerable; (2) the injected row becomes the victim's live production prompt, because /v1/template_versions/latest (templateVersions.ts:24-34) returns `is_draft = false ORDER BY tv.created_at DESC` joined on t.project_id -- so POSTing isDraft:false with attacker-controlled content hijacks the prompt the victim tenant's SDK executes. The lead's secondary claim is also confirmed: templates.ts:99-101 reads `select * from template_version where template_id = ${ctx.params.id}` unscoped, and since `{...undefined}` spreads harmlessly, a cross-tenant PATCH returns the victim's version content even though the scoped UPDATE matched nothing (cross-tenant read). The graph's reachable_from_entry=false is not credible evidence against this: it is false for the correctly-scoped update at line 91 and for the `templates.post` registration call itself, confirming the documented arrow-function under-linking across this entire router file. exploit_search was unavailable (corpus not indexed), which is immaterial -- this is a first-party authorization logic flaw, not a versioned-dependency CVE."
