        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "NO ROLE/ADMIN AUTHORIZATION on privileged organization and billing routes. I swept the entire backend for any role/privilege check and found exactly ONE: `currentUser.role !== \"admin\"` at users.ts:166 (remove-user). The codebase does assign roles (auth/index.ts:61 `role: \"admin\"` for the org creator, auth/index.ts:123 `role: \"member\"` for joiners) and exposes them (users.ts:39 selects `role`), so the privilege model exists but is enforced in only one place. Consequently these privileged handlers authenticate but never authorize beyond org membership: `orgs.patch(\"/\")` (orgs.ts:43, renames the org), `orgs.post(\"/upgrade\")` (orgs.ts:102, changes the Stripe billing plan/subscription), `orgs.get(\"/billing-portal\")` (orgs.ts:79, mints a Stripe billingPortal session URL granting access to payment-method and invoice management), and `projects.post(\"/\")` (projects/index.ts:31). Any low-privilege \"member\" can perform org-wide billing and configuration changes.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. The backend assigns roles (auth/index.ts:61 role:\"admin\" for the org creator, auth/index.ts:123 role:\"member\" for joiners) and exposes them (users.ts:39-56 selects/orders by role), but enforces them in exactly ONE place. I verified this two ways: (a) grep over packages/backend for role/admin/Forbidden/ctx.throw, and (b) a graph query over all equality operators in packages/backend mentioning role/admin/member, which returned only 2 rows -- users.ts:166 (`currentUser.role !== \"admin\"`) and countToken.ts:225 (`key === \"role\"`, an unrelated LLM-message field check). No role check exists upstream either: authMiddleware (auth/utils.ts:81-139) only verifies the JWT and sets ctx.state.userId/orgId, and its SessionData interface (lines 56-60) has no `role` field at all, so role enforcement is impossible without the DB lookup that only users.ts performs. Consequently these privileged handlers authenticate but never authorize beyond org membership: orgs.patch(\"/\") at orgs.ts:43-53 (renames the org), orgs.get(\"/billing-portal\") at orgs.ts:79-100 (mints a Stripe billingPortal session URL for the org's stripe_customer, granting payment-method/invoice/subscription-cancellation access), orgs.post(\"/upgrade\") at orgs.ts:102-185 (stripe.subscriptions.update + `update org set plan`), and projects.post(\"/\") at projects/index.ts:31-68 (only a free-plan quota check at line 41). On exploitability I did NOT downgrade on the graph's reachable_from_entry=false: that flag is false even for orgs.get(\"/\"), an endpoint the frontend demonstrably calls (dataHooks.ts:142), so it is the documented arrow-function/route-registration under-linking artifact. I proved reach in source instead: src/index.ts:34 app.use(authMiddleware) and :41 app.use(v1.routes()) -> api/v1/index.ts:35 v1.use(orgs.routes()) -> router prefix \"/orgs/:orgId\", and the frontend actively calls /orgs/${org.id}/billing-portal (pages/billing/index.tsx:36) and /orgs/${org.id}/upgrade (components/Layout/UpgradeModal.tsx:97). AGGRAVATING FACTOR the analyst missed: /auth/signup is a PUBLIC route (auth/utils.ts:63, RegExp '/auth/.+'), and with signupMethod:\"join\" plus an attacker-supplied orgId (auth/index.ts:117-135) it inserts role:\"member\", verified:true into ANY org and immediately returns a signed JWT with no invite token or admin approval -- so an unauthenticated attacker who learns an org UUID can self-provision a member account and then drive these billing endpoints. CORRECTION to the analyst: the handlers read ctx.state.orgId (from the JWT), not ctx.params.orgId, so there is no cross-org IDOR in orgs.ts; the defect is strictly missing ROLE authorization within the caller's own org. Version/exploit-reference check was inconclusive and immaterial: the exploit corpus is not indexed in this environment and no exact version is pinnable (root package.json is a 1.0.0 monorepo placeholder), but this is a first-party authorization logic flaw, not a dependency CVE."
