        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-942",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The CORS origin allow-list is deliberately neutralized by a hand-rolled wrapper whose own name announces it: `patchedCors`. Rather than configure @koa/cors with a permitted-origin list, the function overrides the library's origin decision with `origin(ctx) { return ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\" }` while setting `credentials: true` \u2014 i.e. it reflects whatever Origin the attacker sends and tells the browser to send cookies/credentials with it, which is precisely the configuration the library's origin option exists to prevent. There is no allow-list, no env-var check, and no comparison against NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL anywhere in the file. The hand-rolled preflight short-circuit above it is additionally broken: it guards on `ctx.method === \"options\"` in lowercase, but Koa normalizes ctx.method to uppercase (\"OPTIONS\"), so that entire branch \u2014 including its own `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <reflected>` / `Allow-Credentials: true` / 204 response \u2014 is unreachable dead code, which is itself evidence this file was patched by hand and not re-verified. This wrapper is installed globally and ahead of auth: packages/backend/src/index.ts registers `app.use(corsMiddleware)` immediately before `app.use(authMiddleware)`, so every authenticated API route is readable cross-origin by any website a logged-in user visits.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: packages/backend/src/utils/cors.ts:17-24 configures @koa/cors with `origin(ctx) { return ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\" }` and `credentials: true`, i.e. unconditional reflection of the attacker-supplied Origin header into Access-Control-Allow-Origin together with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true. I verified there is no allow-list, no env-var gate and no NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL comparison in the file (grep of packages/backend shows NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL only in auth/index.ts:182, orgs.ts:96, users.ts:123). It is installed globally and before auth at index.ts:33-34 (`app.use(corsMiddleware)` then `app.use(authMiddleware)`). Reachability is positively confirmed in the graph, not assumed: the cors({...}) call at cors.ts:17 (uid 3ab065561da06b30df0778d3cf14c09eeca2841b) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. The lead's secondary claim is also true: the hand-rolled preflight guard `ctx.method === \"options\"` (line 6) is dead code because Koa passes req.method through verbatim in uppercase, and I grepped all of packages/backend/src for any `.method =` reassignment or toLowerCase() in the middleware chain ahead of CORS and found none; this does not mitigate anything since @koa/cors emits the same reflected headers on OPTIONS. `createMiddleware` (utils/middleware.ts:6-8) is a no-op passthrough used only by this file, consistent with a hand-applied patch. I corrected the lead's impact claim: auth is Bearer-JWT from localStorage (auth/utils.ts:88; frontend/utils/fetcher.ts:15-18) and grep for cookies|Set-Cookie|withCredentials across packages returns zero matches, so no ambient browser credential exists and an attacker page cannot read authenticated responses on the victim's behalf. The permissive-CORS/origin-reflection defect is genuine and reachable, but its practical impact is bounded, so severity is MEDIUM rather than HIGH. Version grounding was attempted and is non-determinative: @koa/cors ^5.0.0 (package.json:12) could not be pinned exactly (no node_modules/lockfile build present) and exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"; irrelevant here because the flaw is caller configuration, not a library CVE."
