ader: no matches; no nginx/proxy config in the repo normalizes X-Forwarded-For). An attacker therefore rotates the limiter bucket key at will with a single spoofed header, so the 150/min cap imposes no bound on credential guessing against /auth/login. No stricter per-account guard exists either: the auth router is created at auth/index.ts:10-12 with only .post() handlers and no .use() (grep `auth\\.use\\(`: no matches), and /auth/login is exempt from JWT auth via the publicRoutes regex `/auth/.+` (auth/utils.ts:63). ONE CORRECTION to the analyst: their gap (2) does not apply to login \u2014 because /auth/login matches `/auth/.+`, authMiddleware returns early (auth/utils.ts:90-106) without setting ctx.state.userId, so the whitelist (ratelimit.ts:18-23) evaluates false and the limiter DOES run on login. That whitelist is a genuine but separate weakness (any valid JWT holder is unmetered API-wide). The brute-force conclusion rests on gaps (1)+(3), both confirmed. On reachability: the graph marks the auth.post(\"/login\") call reachable_from_entry=false / hop_distance=-1, but that is the documented route-registration/arrow-function under-linking artifact \u2014 the router is mounted at index.ts:42 `app.use(auth.routes())` on a server listening at index.ts:46, and the graph itself lists packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts::program:verifyPassword (the function called at auth/index.ts:155 inside the login handler) as an EntryPoint with exposure=\"reachable\". /auth/login is an unauthenticated, internet-facing endpoint, so I did not downgrade on the stale static label. Advisory exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", giving no version signal; immaterial since this is first-party application logic rather than a dependency CVE."
