            "text": "UNAUTHENTICATED, UNTHROTTLED EMAIL DISPATCH WITH ATTACKER-CONTROLLED RECIPIENT AND DISPLAY NAME. `users.post(\"/send-verification\")` (users.ts:126) is listed in authMiddleware's publicRoutes (auth/utils.ts:75 pushes the exact string \"/v1/users/send-verification\"), and its handler validates only the SHAPE of the input via zod (`z.object({ email: z.string().email(), name: z.string() })`) before calling `sendVerifyEmail(email, name)`. There is no authentication, no proof the caller owns the address, no per-address send counter or cooldown, and `name` is unconstrained free text that gets rendered into the outgoing message. The only throttle is the global 150/min IP limiter, which lead #13 shows is bypassable via X-Forwarded-For because index.ts:26 sets `app.proxy = true`. This permits using the product's own reputable mail domain for email-bombing a third party and for delivering attacker-authored text to arbitrary inboxes. A closely related missing-validation issue: `users.get(\"/verify-email\")` (users.ts:81) accepts any JWT bearing an `email` claim via `jwtVerify(token, ...JWT_SECRET)` with no check that the token was minted for verification, so a long-lived session token also satisfies it.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. The endpoint POST /v1/users/send-verification is unauthenticated: auth/utils.ts:75 contains the exact literal \"/v1/users/send-verification\" in publicRoutes, the match at auth/utils.ts:84-86 is strict `route === ctx.path`, and auth/utils.ts:90 calls `await next()` when no bearer token is present. The path resolves exactly: index.ts:41 `app.use(v1.routes())` + api/v1/index.ts:19 prefix \"/v1\" + api/v1/index.ts:36 `v1.use(users.routes())` + users.ts:10 prefix \"/users\" + route \"/send-verification\". The handler (users.ts:126-136) validates only the SHAPE of input via zod and then calls `sendVerifyEmail(email, name)` with no DB lookup, no proof the caller owns the address, no check that an account exists, and no per-address counter or cooldown. That reaches a live outbound send: emails.ts:9-19 -> CONFIRM_EMAIL (emails.ts:26-43) sets `to: [email]` and `from: GENERIC_SENDER` -> sendEmail.ts:6-13 POSTs to https://api.resend.com/emails. The sole throttle is the global koa-ratelimit of max:150/60s keyed on `ctx.request.ip` (ratelimit.ts:10,16), and index.ts:26 sets `app.proxy = true`, so Koa derives request.ip from the leftmost X-Forwarded-For value, making the bucket attacker-rotatable and the limiter effectively bypassable. Net effect: any unauthenticated party can drive unbounded mail from the product's own reputable sender domain to an arbitrary third-party recipient (email bombing / brand abuse / quota burn). TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (1) the handler ends at line 136, not 137; (2) the attacker-controlled display text is narrower than claimed -- extractFirstName (emails.ts:4-7) does `name.split(\" \")[0]`, so only a space-free token is rendered into the body (arbitrary length, newlines and URLs still survive), which limits but does not eliminate the attacker-authored-text angle. The graph marks every call at users.ts:126/131/133 `reachable_from_entry:false, hop_distance:-1`, but that is the documented arrow-function-handler under-linking -- the source proves the route is mounted on the HTTP surface, so I did not downgrade on it. The analyst's secondary claim also holds but is lower impact: signJwt (auth/utils.ts:33-46) sets no typ/audience/purpose claim and session tokens carry `email` (auth/index.ts:98-102, 128-132), so a 1-month session token satisfies users.ts:88 -- however the email claim is the caller's own, so it only self-verifies one's own account. exploit_search was not applicable: this is first-party application logic with no versioned component, and the XFF behavior is documented Koa semantics under app.proxy=true, not a CVE."
