          "ruleId": "CWE-640",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The one-time-use protection for password-reset tokens was implemented on the write side and then never wired into the verify side \u2014 it is a fully orphaned control. `auth.post(\"/request-password-reset\")` persists the reset token with `await sql`update account set recovery_token = ${token} where id = ${user.id}`` (line 180), which only has a purpose if the reset endpoint later checks and clears it. But `auth.post(\"/reset-password\")` (line 194) does nothing of the sort: it takes `token` from the request body, calls `verifyJwt<{email}>(token)` to check only the HMAC signature, and immediately runs `update account set password_hash = ${passwordHash} where email = ${email}`. A graph-wide search proves `recovery_token` appears at exactly one site in the entire codebase (the write at line 180) \u2014 it is never SELECTed, never compared, never nulled. Two consequences: (1) reset tokens are infinitely replayable within their 1-hour TTL and are not invalidated by use or by issuing a newer one; (2) far worse, because the check is a bare signature verification over any JWT bearing an `email` claim, every other JWT minted by this app with the same JWT_SECRET is accepted as a password-reset token. `auth.post(\"/login\")` returns `signJwt({userId, email, orgId})` with a one-month default TTL, and `sendVerifyEmail` mints `signJwt({ email })` which is handed out in a plain GET URL (`/v1/users/verify-email?token=...`, itself a public route). So a leaked or logged email-verification link \u2014 or a stolen session token \u2014 can be POSTed to /auth/reset-password to take over that account permanently. The stored recovery_token that would have distinguished a genuine reset token from any other token is sitting in the database, unread.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. The one-time-use control for password-reset tokens is written but never read: a tree-wide grep for `recovery_token|recoveryToken` across /tmp/workspaces/65f020d8fa3dc8b7/tree returns exactly TWO hits \u2014 the column declaration (packages/db/init.sql:31) and the single write (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts:180). There is no SELECT, no comparison, and no nulling anywhere. The verify side, `auth.post(\"/reset-password\")` at auth/index.ts:194-218, takes `token` from the request body, performs only `await verifyJwt<{email}>(token)` (line 203) \u2014 which is a bare HMAC check, `jose.jwtVerify` with JWT_SECRET, auth/utils.ts:48-53 \u2014 and then immediately executes `update account set password_hash = ${passwordHash} where email = ${email} returning *` (line 208), returning a fresh month-long session JWT (211-215). Because `signJwt` (auth/utils.ts:33-46) sets only exp/iat/nbf and no `aud`/`sub`/token-type claim, token purpose is indistinguishable, so ANY app-minted JWT bearing an `email` claim is accepted as a reset credential. Such tokens exist and are exposed: `sendVerifyEmail` mints `signJwt({ email })` with NO expiresIn \u2014 hence the ONE_MONTH default, not 1 hour as the analyst assumed \u2014 and ships it in a plain GET URL `/v1/users/verify-email?token=...` (packages/backend/src/utils/emails.ts:14-16), a route explicitly whitelisted as public (auth/utils.ts:74); login (index.ts:163-167) and signup (98-102, 128-132) likewise mint `{userId, email, orgId}`. Consequences I verified: (1) genuine reset tokens are infinitely replayable for their 1-hour TTL and are invalidated neither by use nor by issuing a newer one; (2) a leaked verification link or stolen session bearer escalates to permanent account takeover via password change. On reachability I explicitly overrode the graph: `reachable_from_entry=false` on all 19 nodes I queried at lines 180/194/203/208 is the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact, contradicted by source \u2014 `app.use(auth.routes())` at packages/backend/src/index.ts:42 mounts the router (prefix \"/auth\", auth/index.ts:11) and `authMiddleware` (src/index.ts:34) matches `new RegExp(\"/auth/.+\")` as a public route (auth/utils.ts:63), so POST /auth/reset-password is reachable unauthenticated. I downgrade severity from the analyst's implied critical to HIGH because exploitation requires possessing some already-minted JWT (JWT_SECRET is not forgeable), not pure anonymous access. The advisory exploit-reference check was not usable: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", and this is first-party logic with no versioned component to compare against a fix boundary."
