          "ruleId": "CWE-307",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The rate limiter is a half-dismantled control whose own comments contradict the live code. `max: 150, // limit unlogged IPs to 50 requests per minute` \u2014 the comment documents the intended threshold as 50 while the enforced value is 150, a 3x silent loosening that the stale comment hides from any reviewer skimming the file. Alongside it, the `blacklist` hook is entirely commented out (`// blacklist: (ctx) => { //   // some logic that returns a boolean // },`) and the `whitelist` body still carries the placeholder remnant `// some logic that returns a boolean` after its `return false`, showing the handler was never finished. The whitelist as written exempts every authenticated request from rate limiting outright (`if (ctx.state.userId) return true`). Worse, the limiter keys on `id: (ctx) => ctx.request.ip || ctx.ip || ctx.state.userId` while packages/backend/src/index.ts sets `app.proxy = true`, which makes Koa derive ctx.request.ip from the client-controlled X-Forwarded-For header \u2014 so an attacker rotating that header gets a fresh 150-request bucket per value. Because this is the only throttle in front of the unauthenticated `/auth/login` and `/auth/request-password-reset` routes (`new RegExp('/auth/.+')` is in publicRoutes, and these run before any userId is set), credential stuffing and reset-email flooding are effectively unthrottled.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. The sole throttle in the backend keys its bucket on `ctx.request.ip` (ratelimit.ts:10) while index.ts:26 sets `app.proxy = true`. In Koa 2.x (koa ^2.15.0, package.json:21) `request.ip` = `this.ips[0] || this.socket.remoteAddress`, and `ips` is the split X-Forwarded-For list only when app.proxy is true; `ips[0]` is the LEFTMOST, client-supplied entry (preserved even behind an appending proxy). I grepped all of packages/backend for proxyIpHeader/maxIpsCount/X-Forwarded-For and the only hit is `app.proxy = true`, so no maxIpsCount clamp forces a trusted right-hand entry. An attacker rotating the header therefore gets a fresh `max: 150`/60s bucket per value. This limiter is the ONLY control in front of unauthenticated auth endpoints: index.ts:34-35 registers authMiddleware before ratelimit; auth/utils.ts:63 puts `new RegExp('/auth/.+')` in publicRoutes, and authMiddleware L84-96 calls next() for public routes WITHOUT setting ctx.state.userId, so the whitelist is false and the spoofable key is all that guards auth.post(\"/login\") (auth/index.ts:139-170) and auth.post(\"/request-password-reset\") (L172-192, one email sent per request). Grep for throttle|lockout|failed_login|loginAttempts|captcha|attempt|429 across packages/backend/src shows no account lockout, attempt counter, or CAPTCHA anywhere. Result: effectively unthrottled credential stuffing and reset-email flooding. Graph corroborates reach: the id callback body (L10) and whitelist body (L20) are reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, while the enclosing object-literal assignments read false -- the documented arrow-function-in-object-property under-linking artifact, so the false labels are not evidence against exploitability. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (1) the stale `max: 150 // ...50 requests` comment and the commented-out blacklist hook are real but cosmetic reviewer-confusion smells, NOT the defect -- the defect is the spoofable key plus app.proxy=true; (2) the advisory exploit_search could not run (corpus not indexed) and no exact dep version is pinnable (no lockfile, no :Dependency koa nodes), which is immaterial since this is application logic, not a dependency CVE -- I claim no CVE. Additional unflagged weakness: the whitelist exempts ALL authenticated requests from rate limiting on every endpoint, so one valid account bypasses throttling entirely."
