            "text": "The password-update page is a stub that was never implemented, yet it reports success to the user. `handlePasswordReset` in packages/frontend/pages/update-password.tsx contains the hedge `// TODO: reset password with supertoken` followed by the hardcoded `const ok = true`, then unconditionally fires a notification titled \"Success\" with message \"Password updated successfully\" and redirects to \"/\" on `if (ok)`. No fetch, no API call, no backend request of any kind occurs \u2014 the submitted password is discarded. The sibling page packages/frontend/pages/reset-password.tsx implements the same flow correctly (`await fetcher.post(\"/auth/reset-password\", { arg: { password, token } })`), which establishes that the working call was expected here and is simply absent. The security consequence is a silent failure of a security-critical user action: a user who believes they have rotated a compromised password (and who may therefore stop treating the old one as exposed, or stop rotating it elsewhere) still has the old credential live. The leftover `const ok = true` with a dead `if (ok)` branch is the signature of a disabled implementation left behind a hardcoded pass.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified in source: handlePasswordReset in packages/frontend/pages/update-password.tsx:34-52 contains `// TODO: reset password with supertoken` (L37) followed by hardcoded `const ok = true` (L38), then unconditionally fires notifications.show with title \"Success\"/message \"Password updated successfully\" (L40-45) and redirects via a dead `if (ok)` branch (L47-49). There is no fetch and no API call anywhere in the function \u2014 the `password` argument is bound and never read again, so the submitted password is silently discarded while the UI affirms success. Corroborating evidence the network call was removed rather than never intended: L14 imports `errorHandler` which is never used, and `fetcher` is not imported at all, whereas the sibling page reset-password.tsx imports fetcher (L16) and uses it (L51) via `await fetcher.post(\"/auth/reset-password\", { arg: { password, token } })`; the backend endpoint it should call exists and correctly persists a hash (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts:194-218). This is a real protection-mechanism failure: a security-critical action reports success without performing it. I DOWNGRADE the analyst's impact framing and severity: contrary to the implication that this is the live reset flow, a full-tree grep for \"update-password\" returns only the file's own definition \u2014 no link, redirect, menu entry, import, or email template points at it, and the password-reset email links to the working /reset-password page (auth/index.ts:182). Layout/index.tsx:21-27 omits /update-password from the auth-page allowlist, so signed-out visitors are pushed to /login (L53-56) and only a signed-in user entering the URL directly reaches the stub. A frontend-wide password grep also shows no account/settings password-change UI exists, so the stub shadows no working feature. The defect is confirmed and the route is live, but it is not attacker-triggerable and no guided flow leads users to it, so real-world impact is limited to false assurance for a user who finds the unlinked URL, plus latent risk if a developer later wires a link assuming it works. exploit_search was not applicable: the finding names no versioned component, only first-party stub logic."
